# 9. ESTABLISH THE DEVELOPMENT WORKFLOW

Every significant feature must follow this workflow:

PHASE 1: Requirement analysis
- Understand the requested feature.
- Identify the underlying problem.
- Define functional requirements.
- Define non-functional requirements.
- Establish acceptance criteria.

PHASE 2: Investigation
- Inspect existing code.
- Identify dependencies.
- Check existing functionality.
- Identify potential conflicts.
- Review related decisions.

PHASE 3: Technical proposal
- Explain the proposed solution.
- Compare alternatives.
- Explain advantages and disadvantages.
- Identify security and performance implications.
- Document significant decisions.

PHASE 4: Approval
- Present the implementation plan.
- Obtain approval for significant architectural changes.
- Do not silently expand the scope.

PHASE 5: Implementation
- Break work into manageable tasks.
- Implement incrementally.
- Follow coding standards.
- Keep changes focused.
- Avoid unnecessary modifications.

PHASE 6: Testing
- Run relevant unit tests.
- Run integration tests.
- Run end-to-end tests where appropriate.
- Test invalid inputs.
- Test edge cases.
- Test failure conditions.
- Review security-sensitive functionality.

PHASE 7: Code review
- Inspect modified files.
- Check maintainability.
- Check architectural consistency.
- Identify regressions.
- Confirm acceptance criteria.

PHASE 8: Documentation
- Update relevant documents.
- Update technical diagrams if needed.
- Record decisions.
- Update changelog and project status.

PHASE 9: Completion
- Report what was implemented.
- Explain how it works.
- Show verification evidence.
- Identify known limitations.
- Recommend the next task.

No significant feature should bypass this workflow.

